Chapter: UriEncode
  cites Encode chapter Uri
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 `uri-encode` per RFC 3986 section 2. It did not exist
 anywhere before this: `Net chapter OAuthClient` called it and nothing
 defined it, which is one of the three reasons that chapter had never
 compiled.

 The last line is the one that matters and the reason this test is not
 just the obvious cases. RFC 3986 percent-encodes OCTETS. A non-ASCII
 character is more than one of them, so an encoder written over
 CHARACTERS emits a single escape where the standard wants two, and no
 server decodes that back to what was sent. U+00E9 is two UTF-8 bytes,
 `C3 A9`, and it must come out as `%C3%A9` rather than `%E9`.

 It is built with `from-unicode` rather than typed into this file so the
 source stays ASCII, and so the test states the code point it means
 instead of depending on what an editor saved.

 The unreserved line is the other half of the pair. An encoder that
 escaped everything would satisfy every case below except that one, and
 would still be wrong in the way that matters least and annoys most.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("unreserved " & uri-encode "abcXYZ019-._~")
    print-line-uni ("reserved " & uri-encode "a b&c=d/e?f")
    print-line-uni ("url " & uri-encode "https://example.com/a b")
    print-line-uni ("utf8 " & uri-encode (char-to-text (code-to-char (from-unicode 233))))
  end
